  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA 1034 hPa anticyclone now resides in the Mid-Atlantic region\nwith large-scale ridging continuing to build eastward from the\ncentral CONUS. Dry weather thus persists across the region with\ntemperatures moderating 5 up to nearly 15 degrees above normal \nin response to heights aloft reaching anomalous magnitudes. In\nfact, latest NAEFS data indicates 850mb to 200mb heights are 2 \nto nearly 2.5 STDEVs above normal through tomorrow and 1 to 2\nSTDEVs above normal through Sunday. The most mild days of the\nnext seven will be Thursday and Friday. On Thursday, this first\nridge crests overhead, allowing temperatures to largely reach\nthe 50s to pockets of low 60s. A weak shortwave will then track\nto our north Thursday night before a reinforced ridge crests\nover us once again on Friday. Highs will be similar to Thursday,\nthough low 60s will be more common throughout a greater portion\nof the Hudson Valley. \n\nMedium- to long-range guidance is beginning to hint at a break\ndown of the current fair weather pattern early next week,\nyielding potential for our first appreciable rainfall in over\nseven days. However, at this time, there is low confidence in\nthis potential given substantial uncertainty in the track of the\ncoastal system which would bring said rainfall. While some\ndeterministic guidance brings the low far enough westward to\ngive at least some areas measurable rainfall, others swing it\nout to sea with little if any precipitation falling across our\nCWA. This will be the system to monitor going forward, so we\nwill provide updates on the details as they become clearer.",
